DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       rrppmmaattcchh()  handles a user response to yes or no questions, with support
       for internationalization.

       _r_e_s_p_o_n_s_e should be a null‐terminated string containing a  user‐supplied
       response, perhaps obtained with ffggeettss(3) or ggeettlliinnee(3).

       The  user’s  language preference is taken into account per the environ‐
       ment variables LLAANNGG, LLCC__MMEESSSSAAGGEESS, and LLCC__AALLLL, if the program has called
       sseettllooccaallee(3) to effect their changes.

       Regardless of the locale, responses matching ^^[[YYyy]] are always  accepted
       as  affirmative,  and those matching ^^[[NNnn]] are always accepted as nega‐
       tive.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       After examining _r_e_s_p_o_n_s_e, rrppmmaattcchh() returns 0 for a recognized negative
       response ("no"), 1 for a recognized positive response ("yes"),  and  -1
       when the value of _r_e_s_p_o_n_s_e is unrecognized.

EERRRROORRSS
       A  return  value  of  -1  may indicate either an invalid input, or some
       other error.  It is incorrect to only  test  if  the  return  value  is
       nonzero.

       rrppmmaattcchh() can fail for any of the reasons that rreeggccoommpp(3) or rreeggeexxeecc(3)
       can  fail;  the error is not available from _e_r_r_n_o or anywhere else, but
       indicates a failure of the regex engine (but  this  case  is  indistin‐
       guishable from that of an unrecognized value of _r_e_s_p_o_n_s_e).

