BBUUGGSS
       The YYEESSEEXXPPRR and NNOOEEXXPPRR of some locales (including "C") only inspect the
       first character of the _r_e_s_p_o_n_s_e.  This can mean that "yno" et al.
       resolve to 11.  This is an unfortunate historical side‐effect which
       should be fixed in time with proper localisation, and should not deter
       from rrppmmaattcchh() being the proper way to distinguish between binary
       answers.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
       The following program displays the results when rrppmmaattcchh() is applied to
       the string given in the program’s command‐line argument.

       #define _DEFAULT_SOURCE
       #include <locale.h>
       #include <stdio.h>
       #include <stdlib.h>
       #include <string.h>

       int
       main(int argc, char *argv[])
       {
           if (argc != 2 || strcmp(argv[1], "--help") == 0) {
               fprintf(stderr, "%s response\n", argv[0]);
               exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
           }

           setlocale(LC_ALL, "");
           printf("rpmatch() returns: %d\n", rpmatch(argv[1]));
           exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);
       }

